//! Mobile-friendly interface to the Mumcord engine, with Kotlin and Swift bindings generated by
//! UniFFI (proc-macro mode).
//!
//! Shape: commands go in as methods of [`MumcordEngine`] (server list, connecting, channels,
//! people, settings), state comes out as a [`BridgeSnapshot`] and as [`BridgeEvent`]s delivered
//! to the app's [`EngineListener`]. The app
//! hands in its secure storage as a [`SecureStorage`]. Engine types never appear in the
//! bindings; the mapping lives in `convert`.
//!
//! # Chat
//!
//! Message content crosses the bridge as [`MessageContent`], a small closed model (text runs
//! with a fixed set of styles, checked `http`/`https` links, quotes, code, images by reference),
//! never as HTML: the engine sanitises what the server sends, and the bridge keeps only what the
//! model knows, so an app cannot be made to interpret markup. History is read in pages
//! ([`MumcordEngine::chat_page`]), new messages arrive as [`BridgeEvent::ChatMessageReceived`],
//! image bytes are fetched on demand, and [`BridgeEvent::ChatNotification`] names the messages
//! worth a system notification. Names, message text and addresses are never logged.
//!
//! # Threads
//!
//! The bridge owns one tokio runtime. `async` methods run on it and never block the caller.
//! [`MumcordEngine::new`] opens the database and reads the secure storage, so apps call it off
//! the main thread. Events arrive in order on one bridge thread (`mumcord-bridge-events`), never
//! on an audio thread. [`SecureStorage`] is called from the constructor's thread and from
//! runtime threads.
//!
//! # Audio
//!
//! The app captures and plays the audio itself (only the system's voice-communication session
//! gives echo cancellation and call routing) and moves it through four C functions that its
//! real-time audio threads call directly: `bridge/include/mumcord_audio.h`, with the handles of
//! [`MumcordEngine::audio_endpoints`]. [`AudioWanted`] ([`BridgeEvent::AudioWantedChanged`],
//! [`BridgeSnapshot::audio`]) says when to open the microphone and the speaker.
//!
//! # Android start-up
//!
//! Checking certificates against the system store needs the app context once per process, before
//! the first [`MumcordEngine`] is created. UniFFI cannot pass a JNI context, so this is a plain
//! JNI function next to the generated bindings. The app declares
//!
//! ```kotlin
//! package app.mumcord.mobile.platform.securestore
//!
//! object SystemCertificates {
//!     external fun init(context: android.content.Context)
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! loads the library (`System.loadLibrary("mumcord_mobile")`) and calls
//! `SystemCertificates.init(applicationContext)`. The app also has to ship the Kotlin companion
//! library of `rustls-platform-verifier` (Maven artifact `org.rustls:rustls-platform-verifier`
//! in the version of the `rustls-platform-verifier-android` crate, from the Maven repository
//! named in that crate's README). iOS needs no start-up call.
//!
//! # Bindings
//!
//! ```text
//! cargo build -p mumcord-mobile-bridge
//! cargo run -p mumcord-mobile-bridge --features bindgen --bin uniffi-bindgen -- generate \
//!     --library <target dir>/debug/libmumcord_mobile.so --language kotlin --out-dir <dir>
//! ```
//!
//! and the same with `--language swift`. Kotlin package `app.mumcord.mobile.bridge`, Swift
//! module `MumcordBridge` (`uniffi.toml`).

